concepts.body:
Spring Boot reads configuration from `src/main/resources/application.properties` (or `application.yml`). Hundreds of built-in properties tune auto-configured components: `server.port=8081`, `spring.datasource.url=...`, `logging.level.org.springframework.web=DEBUG`, `spring.jackson.serialization.indent-output=true`.

Values can come from many sources, and later ones override earlier ones. The most important, from lower to higher priority:

- `application.properties` inside the jar;
- `application.properties` next to the jar (or in `./config/`);
- **environment variables**, using relaxed binding: `SERVER_PORT=9000` sets `server.port`;
- command-line arguments: `java -jar app.jar --server.port=9000`.

This is how one artifact runs everywhere: defaults in the jar, environment-specific values injected by the platform.

Your own settings work the same way. Inject a single value with `@Value("${app.greeting}")`, with a default after a colon: `@Value("${app.retries:3}")`. Properties can reference others: `app.url=http://${app.host}:${server.port}`. For anything more than one or two values, prefer typed `@ConfigurationProperties` classes (a later lesson).

Never commit secrets in `application.properties`; supply them through environment variables or a secret manager.

